  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWith the central US ridge retrograding back to the west, the\ntrough that is over New England is moving into the eastern Great\nLakes region. While it is still on the warmer side today, dry\nair aloft is mixing down with dew points steadily falling early\nthis afternoon. There is still a small chance at some shower and\nthunderstorm development, mainly for the far western Twin Tiers\ninto NEPA. Cooler air aloft tomorrow and still some residual\nmoisture could result in a couple of showers developing in the\nafternoon, mainly over the Catskills. The Saturday through at\nleast Tuesday, surface high pressure builds in as well likely\nkeeping the region dry. Day time highs will be right around\nseasonable averages with morning fog developing in the valleys.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nWednesday into Thursday next week will have a deeper shortwave\ntrough progressing through the Northern Tier of the US. Ensemble\nmember MSLP means and low pressure centers are hinting at a\nstronger surface low developing and passing north of the Great\nLakes. With most of the ensemble members keeping the low to the\nnorth, not much precipitation is anticipated outside of when the\ncold front moves through. Deterministic models do have a stream\nof higher PWATs and dew points advecting north ahead of the\nfront so depending on timing of the fronts passage, there could\nbe some stronger storms along it.",
